  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES satellite reveals the dry slot of a deep mid-level in \nnorthern Ontario trough draped across the UP and Lake Superior. At \nthe surface, strong pressure rises have been observed in the western \nlake behind a departing low pressure system. This, and efficient \nmixing into strong winds aloft, have been able to create sustained \nwinds 25-35 mph in the Keweenaw with 45-55 mph gusts measured at \nCMX. A Windy Advisory for N Houghton and Keweenaw Counties remains \nin effect through this evening. Tonight, winds slowly diminish as \nthe pressure gradient slackens with incoming high pressure. Under \nmostly clear skies and decoupling winds from the boundary layer, \nsome patchy fog is possible across area of the interior. Temps fall \ninto the 40s with low 50s likely near the lakeshores and upper 30s \nor the interior west and central.\n\nHeaded into the rest of the week, seasonable to slightly above \nnormal temperatures will be the rule under mainly zonal flow aloft. \nSome weak shortwave action may touch off some light rain activity \nlate Wednesday into Thursday. Otherwise, the next best shot at more \nwidespread precipitation returns sometime in the Friday/Saturday \ntimeframe as ascent ramps up ahead of a lifting warm front. At this \ntime, most deterministic models suggest the bulk of the rain remains \nsouth across WI and their ensembles counterparts / the NBM point \ntowards potentially meaningful rain (50% chance for >0.5\") across \nthe south half of the UP.",
